title: 'State sync: streams chunks directly to database'
doc:
- audience: Node Operator
  description: |-
    State sync writes each verified chunk to the database as it arrives instead of holding the
    whole state in memory (#5053).
    Fast sync and warp sync both download the state of one target block, and a restart resumes
    that download from the recorded cursor. After a long stop the target can be too far below the
    last finalized block: peers keep the state of the last 256 finalized blocks by default and
    refuse an older one. What happens then depends on the sync mode. Fast sync downloads the
    blocks from the old target up to the last finalized block. Warp sync starts the
    whole warp sync over: a parachain when the target is 256 blocks or more below the finalized
    head, other chains once three state requests in a row fail. Both discard the old download.
    If the state sync of a warp sync fails, the node now stops with an error instead of falling
    back to a full sync that its database, which has no state, cannot run.
    `--sync fast-unsafe` is a deprecated alias of `--sync fast`: the state is always downloaded
    with proofs.
- audience: Node Dev
  description: |-
    The sync strategies write each verified chunk through the new
    `sc_client_api::PartialStateImport` trait, implemented by `Client`, which forwards to the
    backend's `PartialStateStore` from the new `Backend::partial_state()` (`None` by default: such
    a backend cannot state-sync). The database records the target and the cursor, so a restart
    resumes the download, and the backend discards one that never completed. A warp sync that
    starts over calls `abandon_partial_state` first, so no restart resumes the old target. Each
    node position is written exactly once, as parity-db's reference counting needs. The target
    block imports with the `ImportedState` marker, now a unit struct, and
    `BlockImportOperation::mark_state_synced`.
    `SyncMode::LightState` loses `skip_proofs`, `ProofProvider::verify_range_proof` also returns
    the decoded trie nodes, and the syncing strategies require `PartialStateImport` of the client.
    Supersedes #9247.
